## Step 4: Determine Next Experiment

**Key observations:**
- The highest confirmed (multi-run) modulus region is around 17.5 wt% polymer, bath_temp=10°C, short wait time, N2=True — but with high run-to-run variability
- The highest PF candidate (19p5-25deg-130s-NoN2-1550s at 0.4295) has only one valid measurement and run2 failed completely
- The 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s at 476.8/0.3931 has a top-edge gap defect
- The 17p5-20deg-90s-N2-1250s at 683.3/0.2824 is a strong modulus candidate but unconfirmed (run2 failed)

**Priority analysis:**
- **19p5-25deg-130s-NoN2-1550s**: Highest PF (0.4295) on the Pareto front, but run2 produced zero mechanical measurements. This is the most important unvalidated Pareto candidate. However, run2 also had very poor fit quality (all 4 specimens failed). The conditions may be inherently unreliable for mechanical characterization.
- **17p5-20deg-90s-N2-1250s**: 683.3/0.2824 is a strong balanced candidate (high modulus, moderate PF), good membrane quality in run1, but run2 failed. This is a high-priority validation target.
- **Exploration**: The region around polymer_wt=18–19, bath_temp=5–10°C, moderate wait times, N2=True has shown promising modulus values. The combination of higher polymer concentration (which tends to increase modulus) with conditions that also allow higher PF (lower bath temp, moderate wait) hasn't been fully explored.

**Decision**: The 17p5-20deg-90s-N2-1250s candidate (683.3 bar / 0.2824) is a strong Pareto-front candidate with good membrane quality in run1 but run2 failed to produce any mechanical data. This needs validation. However, I should also consider whether a new exploration point could be more informative.

Looking at the data pattern:
- Higher bath temp (15-25°C) tends to give higher PF but lower modulus
- Lower bath temp (5°C) with N2 and moderate polymer wt (17.5-18.5) gives higher modulus
- The 18-15deg-600s condition gave 476.8/0.3931 — a good PF with decent modulus, but had a top-edge gap

The **17p5-20deg-90s-N2-1250s** candidate (run1: 683.3/0.2824, good quality; run2: failed) needs a third run to confirm. This is a Pareto-front candidate with a large apparent gain in modulus and good membrane quality in run1, but run2 failure creates high uncertainty. A validation repeat is warranted.

